Step 1: Analyze the rightmost column first.
In the given addition problem, we see that $A_6+B_6+5_6$ gives us a number with a units digit of $2_6$ and a possible carry of 1. Since $A_6 + B_6$ must be 3 in base $6$ to produce this in base $6$, we need to have one of $A_6,B_6$ equal to $6_6$ so that when added, it will produce a units digit of $3$ in base $6$. We need to check if this is possible. The units place and our potential solution of $B=6_6$ would look like below

\begin{array}{c@{}c@{\;}c@{}c@{}c@{}c}
 & & &  &  & A_6 \\
 & & &  &  & B_6 \\
& & &  &  & 5_6\\ \cline{2-6}
 & & &  &  & \mathbf{2_6}\\ \end{array}
Since that doesn't look right because there is an extra digit in our answer, we are thrown off here and must check that the hundreds digit of our potential answer $1$ is possibly going to be correct. If we make $B$ greater than 6, that would give us a place carry over where would be greater than 5 times 6 to be carried over over to the next column therefore it would get rounded down to a 6 to account for the new number. However $B$ is limited because it is the same value as the carry from the next column that this would make. Looking at the hundreds column $B + 1 + 1$ gives us C in the units place of base $6$ and the carry value of $C_6$ at the ten place. In order to have a carry at the ten's column we need that this $C_6$ should be such that it's equal to the highest value for this $C_6$ - having this carry provide means that either it: 
$$B+2 > 5\cdot 6 \quad  \text{and} \quad B +1>5 $$
or
$$B+2 \leq 5\cdot6\quad \text{and} \quad B+1 \leq 5$$

First we consider the last case because its seems like we can get the right possible solution that provides a value for A easily. Therefore,
this would read:
\begin{equation*}
\begin{array}{c@{}c@{\;}c@{}c@{}c@{}c}
 & & & B & B+1 & A \\
 & & &  & 1 & B+1\\
&  & 4 & 1 & 5 & 6\\ \cline{3-6}
 & & & & C & 2. \end{array}
Since our calculation gives the carry of 1 our column now looks like this

\begin{equation*}
\begin{array}{c@{}c@{\;}c@{}c@{}c@{}c}
 & & & B & B+1 & A \\
 & & &  & 1 & B+1\\
&  & 4 & 1 & 5 & 6\\ \cline{3-6}
 & & 1 & C & 5 & 12_6\\
\end{array}
this tells us that the highest possible value for $C_6$  $C = 0 $ therefore giving $B+1=5$. After checking that $B$ is actually one less than $B+1$ which equals to $4$ we obtain the values of $A$ and $B$ as follows:
\begin{array}{c@{}c@{\;}c@{}c@{}c@{}c}
